# Logs of the LaunchAgent runs (daily 06:15, pre-reset backfill 04:00) — local only.
.update.log

# Scheduled-job state, meaningful only on the machine that ran it: the last
# usage/reset reading (weekly_usage.py falls back to it when the CLI's access
# token has lapsed) and one empty file per already-sent alert (alert.sh dedupes
# on it, see ALERT_REPEAT_HOURS).
#
# No trailing slashes: tools/nightly_worktree.sh gives the worktree a SYMLINK to
# each of these so there is one copy in the main checkout, and a pattern ending
# in / does not match a symlink. Whatever that loop shares belongs here, spelled
# the same way it is spelled there.
.usage_cache.json
.alert-state
.claude
# How many cold solves each unpublished puzzle has already burned, so a grid
# that cannot be solved stops being attempted (see SOLVE_ATTEMPTS_MAX in
# daily_update.sh). Written straight to the main checkout rather than shared by
# symlink, because a count that lives in the nightly worktree is a count the
# worktree rebuild throws away.
.solve_attempts.json
# The published answers a blind annotation is currently hiding, and the only
# copy of them while it runs (tools/blind_annotate.py). Untracked because it
# exists for the length of one claude call and is deleted by the grading pass;
# committed, it would publish the very key the run is pretending not to have.
.blind/
# Everything the pre-reset backfill leaves behind: its log, its run lock, and the
# two rates it measures for itself (.prereset_rate, .prereset_yield). One glob so
# the next thing it learns to measure cannot arrive untracked-but-committable.
.prereset*
puzzles/__pycache__/
tools/__pycache__/
__pycache__/

# Grid-filler word data: ~26MB of MIT-licensed lexicon fetched by
# tools/fetch_lexicon.sh, plus one file derived from it. Reproducible in
# under a minute, so it is fetched rather than committed — see tools/data/README.md.
#
# tools/data/lexicon.tsv is NOT ignored, though it is derived from these too. It
# decides a third of every difficulty rating (difficulty.obscurity), and those
# ratings are committed in puzzles/index.json: while it was fetched, the score a
# puzzle got depended on whether the machine running --reindex happened to have
# it. 4.9MB, MIT, notice in tools/data/README.md.
tools/data/*.js
tools/data/clueability.tsv

# Blind-grading packets built by tools/grade_clues.py. They quote 60 published
# newspaper clues out of the georgeho corpus, which carries no declared licence,
# so they stay local.
#
# This comment used to claim that re-running with the same --seed reproduces the
# same packets. It does not, and believing it cost us a controlled comparison:
# the seeded shuffle depends on the rival pool, so fixing a bug that changed one
# answer's eligible rivals silently moved the A/B/C/D labels for every answer
# after it, and the round-one key was overwritten by a round-two key that looked
# exactly like it. Rounds are now archived under grading/runs/<runid>/ and
# score_grading.py refuses to join scores whose run it cannot find. Nothing here
# is regenerable from a seed — if a round is not archived, it is gone.
tools/data/grading/

# Minute Cryptic reference corpus captured by tools/fetch_minutecryptic.js: 55
# worked examples with their full hint ladders, plus an archive of their daily
# clues. Somebody else's copyrighted teaching material with no declared licence,
# same posture as tools/data/grading/ — kept locally to read and learn from,
# never committed, republished, or copied into a puzzle file.
tools/data/minutecryptic/

# Wrangler's local cache. It holds the Cloudflare account id and account name,
# which are not secrets but are nobody else's business, and this repo is public.
# Deleting it costs one extra prompt on the next deploy.
#
# Pathless, so it matches at any depth: wrangler writes this beside whatever
# directory it was invoked from, not beside wrangler.toml, and `sync/.wrangler/`
# missed the copy tools/reports.py made at the repo root. The scheduled jobs
# `git add -A`, so an ignore rule that only covers the place we happened to look
# is one `cd` away from publishing the account id.
.wrangler/

# One-shot scratch. A leading underscore in tools/ means nothing may depend on it.
# Covers the annotation payloads (tools/_ann_<id>.json) that apply_annotations.py
# consumes, which daily_update.sh deletes at the end of a run — the throwaway
# annotator scripts they replaced were ignored too, and nothing swept them, so
# they accumulated one per puzzle.
tools/_*

# The CLI's per-checkout settings and permissions. Local, machine-specific,
# and reached from the scheduled jobs' worktrees by symlink — which is the
# reason it is named here: those jobs `git add -A`, and an untracked symlink
# pointing at somebody's home directory is not a thing to publish.
.claude/

# The crawlable half of the site, written by tools/build_seo_pages.py: one page
# per puzzle, the archive hub, the lesson, the glossary and the sitemap. Every
# one of them carries a content-hashed ?v= stamp, so an app.js or style.css
# change rewrites all 1,059 — which made a one-line fix a four-figure commit
# with a full rebuild in front of it. Built on a clean checkout and deployed
# straight to Pages by .github/workflows/pages.yml instead.
#
# index.html is deliberately NOT here. The build patches one region of it, but
# it is hand-written, and its own asset stamps are committed by
# tools/stamp_assets.py so the source of the site is readable in the repo.
/puzzles/*/index.html
/puzzles/index.html
/learn/index.html
/abbreviations/index.html
/sitemap.xml

# The per-puzzle social cards, screenshotted by tools/make_og.sh. 24MB of PNGs
# that no page reads from the repo: .github/workflows/pages.yml draws them on the
# clean checkout it publishes, keeping them in an actions/cache between runs and
# redrawing only the ones make_og_card.py's manifest says have changed.
#
# og.png at the root is deliberately NOT here. It is the site card, it is
# deterministic, and its content hash is stamped into the committed index.html.
/og/
